# NOTE ON ANCHORING
# A pattern without a leading slash matches at ANY depth. `jobs/` therefore
# excluded packages/jobs/ as well as the root jobs/ folder it was meant for,
# which silently kept the job registry out of every push. Anything meant to
# match only at the repo root is anchored with a leading slash. Check with:
#   git check-ignore -v <path>

__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
*.pyd

# Virtualenvs — safe unanchored; no source directory is ever called these.
.venv/
venv/
/env/

# Secrets. .env.example is the committed template and must survive.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# .htaccess is SECRET-BEARING on this host. cPanel regenerates it and injects
# the DB password, JWT secret and all three API keys into a `SetEnv` block, so
# a copy pulled down from the server must never be committed.
#
# Caveat: the file is still tracked from the first commit, and .gitignore does
# not apply to tracked files. It is held out of the index with
#   git update-index --skip-worktree .htaccess
# on every clone (local AND server). This line only starts biting if the file
# is ever untracked. The security rules that used to live in it are now in
# deploy/security.htaccess, which IS tracked and carries no secrets.
.htaccess

/build/
/dist/
*.egg-info/

.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

/instance/
*.sqlite3

# Runtime artefacts — root only. `packages/jobs/` is SOURCE and must be committed.
/var/
/jobs/
*.log
*.htaccess